1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,531
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,531 --> 00:00:04,579
In this lecture we study Vector Spaces: A Short Introduction.

3
00:00:04,579 --> 00:00:07,233
Generated with course2video (prototype).

4
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,136
Here is the plan.

5
00:00:09,136 --> 00:00:14,197
We will go through 6 parts: Vectors in R n, Definition of a vector

6
00:00:14,197 --> 00:00:22,171
space, Subspaces, Linear combinations and span, Linear independence and basis and Exercise and solution.

7
00:00:22,771 --> 00:00:26,034
Let's start with part 1: Vectors in R n.

8
00:00:26,634 --> 00:00:31,104
We begin by asking what we actually mean when we write a vector in

9
00:00:31,104 --> 00:00:31,849
R to the n.

10
00:00:31,849 --> 00:00:36,387
A vector u is just an ordered list of n numbers, written (u1,…,un).

11
00:00:36,387 --> 00:00:41,873
The power of linear algebra comes from two very simple operations on these lists.

12
00:00:41,873 --> 00:00:46,546
First, we can add two vectors component by component, so the sum of u

13
00:00:46,546 --> 00:00:48,240
and v is (u1+v1,…,un+vn).

14
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:52,778
Think of sliding the tip of one arrow onto the tail of another; the

15
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:56,164
result points from the origin to the combined tip.

16
00:00:56,164 --> 00:01:01,108
Second, we can scale a vector by a real number a, which simply multiplies

17
00:01:01,108 --> 00:01:03,614
each entry: a·u equals (a u1,…,a un).

18
00:01:03,614 --> 00:01:09,304
This stretches or shrinks the arrow without changing its direction if a is positive.

19
00:01:09,304 --> 00:01:14,316
These operations are the foundation for all later linear‑algebra concepts.

20
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:20,141
Geometrically, in two or three dimensions you can picture these arrows starting at the

21
00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:20,615
origin.

22
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:26,710
For instance, adding (1,2,3) to (4,0,-1) gives (5,2,2) and multiplying (1,2,3) by 2 yields

23
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:27,252
(2,4,6).

24
00:01:27,252 --> 00:01:32,535
A common mistake is to try to add vectors of different lengths; the dimensions

25
00:01:32,535 --> 00:01:33,822
must match exactly.

26
00:01:33,822 --> 00:01:38,225
With these basics in place, we will now see how they fit into the

27
00:01:38,225 --> 00:01:40,798
abstract definition of a vector space.

28
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:45,357
Now we move on to part 2: Definition of a vector space.

29
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:51,022
Having understood addition and scaling in R to the n, the next question is:

30
00:01:51,022 --> 00:01:54,060
what abstract structure captures these ideas?

31
00:01:54,060 --> 00:02:00,408
A vector space is a set V equipped with two operations—addition and scalar multiplication—that

32
00:02:00,408 --> 00:02:02,096
satisfy a list of axioms.

33
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:07,768
The axioms say addition is commutative and associative, there is a zero element that

34
00:02:07,768 --> 00:02:12,494
leaves any vector unchanged, and every vector has an additive inverse.

35
00:02:12,494 --> 00:02:18,167
For scalar multiplication we require that multiplying by 1 leaves a vector alone and

36
00:02:18,167 --> 00:02:21,273
that the zero scalar produces the zero vector.

37
00:02:21,273 --> 00:02:27,012
Crucially, the distributive laws link the two operations; for example a times (u plus

38
00:02:27,012 --> 00:02:28,565
v) equals a u plus a v.

39
00:02:28,565 --> 00:02:34,372
The axioms guarantee that any theorem proved from them applies to all listed examples,

40
00:02:34,372 --> 00:02:38,491
such as Euclidean space, polynomial spaces, or matrix spaces.

41
00:02:38,491 --> 00:02:43,961
Intuitively, they tell us that vectors behave like arrows that we can move around

42
00:02:43,961 --> 00:02:45,649
and stretch consistently.

43
00:02:45,649 --> 00:02:51,389
In the space of 2‑by‑2 matrices, adding matrices and scaling them follows exactly the

44
00:02:51,389 --> 00:02:53,752
same rules as for ordinary vectors.

45
00:02:53,752 --> 00:02:58,546
A subtle point: the zero vector must be the same for both operations, a

46
00:02:58,546 --> 00:03:02,260
detail some forget when checking a new candidate space.

47
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:07,730
Next we will examine special subsets of a vector space that are themselves vector

48
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:08,202
spaces.

49
00:03:08,802 --> 00:03:12,227
Now we move on to part 3: Subspaces.

50
00:03:12,827 --> 00:03:17,358
Now that we know what a vector space is, we ask whether a smaller

51
00:03:17,358 --> 00:03:21,052
collection of vectors can inherit the same structure.

52
00:03:21,052 --> 00:03:25,374
A subspace is simply a subset W of a vector space V that, with

53
00:03:25,374 --> 00:03:31,160
the same addition and scalar multiplication, forms a vector space in its own right.

54
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:36,248
To verify this, we only need three checks: first, W must contain the zero

55
00:03:36,248 --> 00:03:41,337
vector of V; second, it must be closed under addition, meaning the sum of

56
00:03:41,337 --> 00:03:45,728
any two vectors in W stays in W; third, it must be closed under

57
00:03:45,728 --> 00:03:51,235
scalar multiplication, so multiplying any vector in W by any scalar stays in W.

58
00:03:51,235 --> 00:03:58,066
These three conditions are sufficient because all other axioms are automatically inherited from V.

59
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:03,015
For example, the set W consisting of all points (t,2t) as t ranges over

60
00:04:03,015 --> 00:04:08,103
the real numbers satisfies the three conditions, so it is a subspace of R

61
00:04:08,103 --> 00:04:08,661
squared.

62
00:04:08,661 --> 00:04:13,610
A common mistake is to forget to verify that the zero vector belongs to

63
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:18,141
W; the line y equals 2x plus 1 fails this test because its points

64
00:04:18,141 --> 00:04:19,883
never include the origin.

65
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:25,111
Understanding subspaces prepares us for later ideas such as span and basis.

66
00:04:25,711 --> 00:04:30,390
Now we move on to part 4: Linear combinations and span.

67
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:35,670
Now that we’ve seen what a subspace looks like, let’s ask: given a collection

68
00:04:35,670 --> 00:04:39,743
of vectors, what is the full set of vectors we can build from them?

69
00:04:39,743 --> 00:04:41,141
The answer is the span.

70
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:45,396
Think of each vector as a direction you can travel, and the scalars as

71
00:04:45,396 --> 00:04:48,374
how far you can stretch or shrink that direction.

72
00:04:48,374 --> 00:04:54,270
By adding together these stretched directions you reach everything the original set can generate.

73
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:59,619
For instance, take the two unit vectors along the x‑axis and y‑axis in three‑dimensional

74
00:04:59,619 --> 00:04:59,984
space.

75
00:04:59,984 --> 00:05:03,692
If you scale the first by any real number x and the second by

76
00:05:03,692 --> 00:05:07,764
any real number y, then add the results, you obtain a vector (x, y,

77
00:05:07,764 --> 00:05:07,947
0).

78
00:05:07,947 --> 00:05:12,749
That shows every point in the xy‑plane can be expressed as a linear combination

79
00:05:12,749 --> 00:05:16,213
of those two vectors, so the plane is exactly their span.

80
00:05:16,213 --> 00:05:20,772
Notice a subtle point: if you start with just one non‑zero vector, the span

81
00:05:20,772 --> 00:05:24,176
is only a line through the origin, not the entire space.

82
00:05:24,176 --> 00:05:28,188
This idea of reach will be key later when we look for the smallest

83
00:05:28,188 --> 00:05:32,686
set of vectors that still spans the whole space, leading us to the concept

84
00:05:32,686 --> 00:05:33,354
of a basis.

85
00:05:33,354 --> 00:05:38,278
In short, the span captures everything you can reach by stretching and adding the

86
00:05:38,278 --> 00:05:39,129
given vectors.

87
00:05:39,729 --> 00:05:44,477
Now we move on to part 5: Linear independence and basis.

88
00:05:45,077 --> 00:05:50,366
Having understood how vectors can generate a whole subspace, we now ask: when is

89
00:05:50,366 --> 00:05:52,878
a set of vectors free from redundancy?

90
00:05:52,878 --> 00:05:55,457
The answer lies in linear independence.

91
00:05:55,457 --> 00:06:00,151
A set is independent precisely when the only way to combine them to get

92
00:06:00,151 --> 00:06:03,324
the zero vector is to use all zero coefficients.

93
00:06:03,324 --> 00:06:08,084
In other words, no vector can be written as a combination of the others.

94
00:06:08,084 --> 00:06:13,241
This means each vector contributes a new direction that isn’t already covered.

95
00:06:13,241 --> 00:06:18,001
A basis is then defined as an independent set that also spans the entire

96
00:06:18,001 --> 00:06:18,397
space.

97
00:06:18,397 --> 00:06:22,827
It is the minimal recipe of directions that can build every vector.

98
00:06:22,827 --> 00:06:28,182
For example, in the plane the two standard unit vectors are independent and their

99
00:06:28,182 --> 00:06:33,471
linear combinations fill the whole plane, so they form a basis and the dimension

100
00:06:33,471 --> 00:06:33,934
is two.

101
00:06:33,934 --> 00:06:39,024
A common mistake is to assume that any spanning set is automatically a basis;

102
00:06:39,024 --> 00:06:41,272
you must also verify independence.

103
00:06:41,272 --> 00:06:46,958
Remember that the standard basis in n‑dimensional space consists of the n vectors with

104
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:51,784
a single 1 and the rest zeros, confirming that the dimension of ℝⁿ equals

105
00:06:51,784 --> 00:06:51,916
n.

106
00:06:52,516 --> 00:06:56,696
Now we move on to part 6: Exercise and solution.

107
00:06:57,296 --> 00:07:01,374
Let’s put those definitions to work with a quick exercise.

108
00:07:01,374 --> 00:07:06,086
We are given the two vectors (1,2) and (3,6) in the plane and asked

109
00:07:06,086 --> 00:07:08,758
to check whether they are independent.

110
00:07:08,758 --> 00:07:13,048
To test this we look for scalars a and b, not both zero, such

111
00:07:13,048 --> 00:07:17,900
that a times the first vector plus b times the second equals the zero

112
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:18,393
vector.

113
00:07:18,393 --> 00:07:22,472
If we try a equals 3 and b equals -1, we compute 3·(1,2) -

114
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:25,214
1·(3,6) which gives (3‑3, 6‑6) = (0,0).

115
00:07:25,214 --> 00:07:30,629
Since the coefficients (3, -1) are not both zero, we have found a non‑trivial

116
00:07:30,629 --> 00:07:36,185
combination that yields the zero vector, so the vectors are linearly dependent.

117
00:07:36,185 --> 00:07:41,107
Because one is simply a scalar multiple of the other, they only span a

118
00:07:41,107 --> 00:07:44,061
line through the origin, not the whole ℝ².

119
00:07:44,061 --> 00:07:49,335
The key subtle point here is that in ℝ² two vectors are independent exactly

120
00:07:49,335 --> 00:07:52,781
when they are not scalar multiples of each other.

121
00:07:52,781 --> 00:07:58,759
This exercise reinforces the definitions of dependence and span that we just covered.

122
00:07:59,359 --> 00:08:02,233
That brings us to the end of this lecture.

123
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:08,187
We covered Vectors in R n, Definition of a vector space, Subspaces, Linear combinations

124
00:08:08,187 --> 00:08:12,704
and span, Linear independence and basis and Exercise and solution.

125
00:08:12,704 --> 00:08:14,278
Thank you for watching.
